00:44:18.080 --> 00:44:37.139
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): And I will show you the different kind of strategies later. So now, a little word about the game we play, and I'm happy to go into details here, but it's kind of an interesting piece of mathematics. So it's not bulletproof.

278
00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:52.980
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): And do not look at these variables and all these levers in equations as constants, they are not. It's rather about the mechanism, how the game works. And this is then a very good thing to model in game theory.

279
00:44:53.420 --> 00:45:13.059
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): And I want to start with what kind of game we're playing, and this is something I currently see as a rather big mistake everyone is doing. So if you have read your U.S. President's recent note about what is needed in terms of

280
00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:17.870
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): guidelines or guardrails for AI development.

281
00:45:17.870 --> 00:45:26.400
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): And that we probably should not slow it down, but we only need a strong person, like a strong president.

282
00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:40.000
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): and then that everyone needs to fight against a partner that does it anyway, like China, we are then in a complete wrong game. And that's maybe the most important takeaway I want to…

283
00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:50.240
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): give you in this special topic here. So, the temptation is very high to see this as a prisoner's dilemma game. That is one of the most.

284
00:45:50.240 --> 00:46:07.549
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): famous games in game theory, where two parties act simultaneously. They can cooperate or defect. If both would cooperate, the value would be very high, but like prisoners that have to blame someone else.

285
00:46:07.820 --> 00:46:25.610
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): to get a lower sentence, they would probably not cooperate. And this is what we call the Nash Equilibrium, which is a situation you will find yourself in that is stable, it's a dominant strategy, but unfortunately.

286
00:46:25.610 --> 00:46:38.320
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): it leads to a very low, utility, so the combined utility will be very low. And that's exactly, unfortunately, also, what we're doing, this political debate now, Mr. Trump. So, if you have

287
00:46:38.320 --> 00:46:48.829
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): your only guardrails or strategy is a tit-for-tat strategy, and that you have to force developments before someone else does it. You're trapped in this.

288
00:46:48.830 --> 00:47:01.309
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): Prisoner's Dilemma. And that's also the case if you look at it in data sharing. If you think that I cannot give all my private data to Google, because they will do

289
00:47:01.310 --> 00:47:04.759
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): it's too much business with it, and I don't get something back.

290
00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:23.880
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): then it's not a very good strategy. And the opposite, like, the big corporations that only harvest your data and earn a fortune of it, what's probably happening, or happened so far, is also not a very good strategy, because they're all not an optimum outcome.

291
00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:43.359
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): So, we need to look at this from another game's perspective, and that's rather the theories of the trust games. And the difference, the rules of the games, they're quite different. So, in a trust game, we have multiple iterations. We don't only look at one iteration.

292
00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:56.439
Daniel Glinz (Glinz & Company GmbH): And we have, of course, information asymmetry. And that's what we always have, but in trust even more. And what we also have is we have some noise.

293
